## Laterally Engineering Ln-MOFs Epitaxial Heterostructures for Spatially Resolved Planar 2D Photonic Barcoding
## Introduction
Development of photonic heterostructures composed of color-varying structural blocks has drawn increasing attention from the viewpoints of both fundamental research and potential applications.[1] Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), structurally composed of rigid metal nodes and coordinated organic ligands,[2] serve as interesting photonic heterostructured platforms[3] since such media simultaneously possess excellent stability of inorganic materials[4] and great flexibility of organic materials.[5] In the family of MOFs, lanthanide-based MOFs (LnMOFs) are recognized as a unique class of narrow-line light emitters, where Ln ions are structurally isolated by organic ligands thus energy transfer among adjacent Ln ions is prohibited for highly efficient Ln emission.[2d] Benefitting from isomorphous coordination style, Ln-MOFs epitaxial heterostructures are acquirable by incorporating various species of Ln ions into different structural units.[6] Up to now, several types of Ln-MOFs heterostructures have been developed, showing potential applications in information security encoding with improved security levels beyond traditional Ln-MOFs nanostructures.[7] However, Ln-MOFs heterostructures developed thus far are restrained to be one-dimensional (1D) microrods. In comparison to 1D heterostructure, growth of MOFs along in-plane two dimensions offer one more dimension for information encoding, thus it is of paramount importance to develop lateral Ln-MOFs epitaxial heterostructures.
Development of two-dimensional (2D) planar Ln-MOFs micro/nano-templates with controllable size and specific spatial orientation is the key toward lateral Ln-MOFs heterostructures, which will break the conventional rule of preferential growth along one direction.[8] Up to now, a variety of approaches have been proposed to fabricate 2D MOFs micro/nanosheets with controllable size, such as surface modification, buffer layer precipitation, and interfacial assembly.[9] The prerequisite for successful fabrication of the above 2D MOFs micro/nanosheets is that such MOFs is generally composed of a 2D layered topological network. Namely, two distinct coordination modes or weak interlayer forces exist in such MOFs structures, which can be modulated by adding a modulator to impose kinetic limitations on the growth rate along different crystallographic directions.[3f, 9d, 10] As a result, the above approaches are incapable of modulating Ln-MOFs with robust 3D topological network. Therefore, it is of great significance to develop a universal methodology correlating the self-assembly processes and their underlying topological structure for better manipulation of spatially expanded MOFs heterostructures toward advanced applications.
Herein, a controllable synthetic method based on spatial semi-confinement induced steric-hindrance effect is developed for fabrication of lateral Ln-MOFs heterostructures with tunable dimensions. Channel-directed guest modulator molecules are introduced to regulate the in-plane and out-of-plane growth rates of the Ln-MOFs crystals so that the aspect ratio of the developed lateral Ln-MOFs heterostructures can be effectively modulated with the modulator concentration. The correlation between the topological nanostructure and crystal growth preference is demonstrated in Ln-BTC MOFs with regular square channels to determine the geometry of the resultant morphology. Various Ln-MOFs heterostructures with tunable domains are acquired via a stepwise epitaxial growth procedure, which exhibit domain-controlled emissive colors, thus allowing for definition of 2D photonic barcodes. These findings offer unique opportunities to purposely design highly integrated microstructures and smart devices toward advanced anticounterfeiting applications.
## Results and Discussion
Design and Assembly of Tunable Ln-MOFs
The principle of designing tunable lateral Ln-MOFs heterostructures is displayed in Figure 1a-c. Generally, MOFs microstructures with directional channels are prone to growth along the porous channels in order to reduce the surface energy, forming 1D Ln-MOF microrods (Figure 1a).[11] If the porous channels were blocked with guest molecules that have a relatively large lateral molecular skeleton, the growth preference along the porous channels would be intervented. Such intervention is known as steric-hindrance effects (Figure 1b), imposing kinetic modulation on the growth rate along in-plane and out-of-plane directions.[12] Consequently, the morphology and size of Ln-MOFs would be modulated due to the sterichindrance effects (Figure 1c).
In this work, Ln-BTC MOFs is selected as the parental MOFs model for constructing tunable Ln-MOFs heterostructures due to its excellent PL properties and specific topological network (Figure 1d).[11a, 13] Ln-BTC MOFs exhibit characteristic luminescence of Ln ions due to effective ligand-to-lanthanide energy transfer (Figure S1).[14] The structure has a 3D topological network and crystallizes in a chiral space group $P4_{1}22$ , where the BTC organic ligand links six $T b^{3+}$ ions through three carboxylate groups based on the uniform bidentate coordination modes (Figure S2). Based on this connection mode, $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ ions are connected to form a 1D inorganic helical chain. The Bravais-Friedel-Donnay-Harker (BFDH) crystal morphology calculation based on the crystal structure shows a 1D microrod morphology with the dominant growth along the [001] direction (Figure 1e). The 1D inorganic chains are further linked to each other through phenyl groups of BTC ligands along [100] and [010] directions, leading to impenetrable organic walls and a 3D framework containing square channels. The conspicuous square channel might be able to serve as the topological template for development of expanded microsquare structures (Figure S3). The square channels with size of $6.6\times6.6$ $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ could accommodate appropriate modulators to modulate the dimension of the MOFs structures from microrods to microplates.[15]
Below, 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene (BTB) is chosen as the modulator to create tunable microstructures based on the fact that BTB has the triaxially symmetrical molecular structure of the BTC linker. The BTB molecule has three peripheral benzoic acid groups symmetrically distributed in the phenyl core, which features three long and flexible arms in the molecular skeleton (Figure 1f). The lateral dimension of one arm is calculated to be $4.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , which is smaller than the pore size of the Ln-BTC MOFs $(6.6\times6.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ , Figure S4). The lateral dimension of the whole BTB is determined to be $15.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is larger than the pore size of the Ln-BTC MOFs. Therefore, once the BTB molecules were introduced into the reaction system, these molecules might be partially trapped in the pore channel with one arm and the other two arms would hang on the interface between the exposure surface and external solvent environment. Such structural configuration could significantly increase the steric-hindrance effects and disturb the continuous growth of the MOFs along one preferential direction, leading to a strong tendency to grow into a 2D morphology.
A mixed solution of BTC, $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ and various amounts of BTB in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and deionized water was heated in a capped glass vial (Figure 1g). After aging for 2 hours, different shaped microstructures with green emissive colors were finally obtained (Figure 1h-j), where the morphology depends on the amount of the BTB modulator. In the absence of BTB, the obtained Ln-MOFs appear as 1D microrods with an average length of ${\sim}40~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a width of ${\sim}2~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ (Figure 1h, Figure S5-6). By increasing the amount of BTB to 0.2 equiv (BTB/BTC molar ratio), the particle length is reduced to $\sim15\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , while the width is increased to ${\sim}5~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ (Figure 1i). The squareshaped microplates with the side length of $\sim12\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ were acquired when the amount of BTB was increased to 1 equiv (Figure 1j). The above results suggest that the incorporation of the BTB modulator effectively intervents the growth along [001] direction while promotes the growth along the lateral directions, confirming our design principle stated above. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report on simultaneous regulation of lateral and longitudinal dimensions in Ln-MOFs microstructures. It should be noticed that spherical amorphous phase began to appear with further increase of the BTB content (higher than 2 equiv), which might be the product formed by coordination of the carboxylate groups of BTB linkers to ${\mathsf{T}}{\mathsf{b}}^{3}$ ions (Figure S7-8).
As shown in the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images in Figure 2a-c, the developed Ln-MOF MOFs microrods and microplates have smooth surfaces, which exhibit clear 1D morphology and regular rectangular or square shapes, respectively. The synthesized MOFs microparticles display almost the same Fourier-transform Infrared (FTIR) spectra with the initial microrods (Figure S9), suggesting that these MOFs have the similar chemical composition and coordination structures. The as-prepared Ln-MOFs demonstrate excellent thermostability and high stability under various environments (Figure S10-11), which endow them with practical applications abilities for security labels.[16, 17]
## Mechanism of Morphology Modulation
To investigate the molecular packing mode within 1D microrods and 2D microplates, we firstly simulated the growth crystal plane of Ln-MOFs crystal based on attachment energies using Materials Studio package (Figure S12). The results indicate that the crystal is bound by $\{100\}$ and $\{010\}$ facets on the side surfaces and by (101) and (011) facets on the top surfaces. The consistent powder $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns suggest that the resulting distinct microcrystals have a similar crystal structure (Figure 2d). Further analysis of PXRD pattern, we can find that the lying placed microplates, compared with the microrods, show enhanced (101), (003) and (004) diffraction signal peaks. These results indicate that these facets are gradually exposed, suggesting that the growth along the [001] direction is restricted in microplates.[18] Furthermore, the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images and the corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns in Figure 2e-f indicate that the 1D microrod is a high-quality single crystal growing along the [001] direction while the microplate shows distinct 2D single-crystal growing directions along [110] and [1-10], respectively. The strong (hk0) diffraction spots indicate that the 2D microcrystals are prone to growing along the [hk0] crystallographic directions.[19]
The results indicate that the morphology of the developed Ln-BTC MOFs microstructures strongly depends on the growth kinetics, especially the relative rate of [001] and [100] or [010] crystallographic directions, which could be effectively modulated by the modulator during the self-assembly process.[20] Based on the above analysis, [001] is the prominent crystal growth direction in the absence of the modulator. Introduction of BTB molecules into the pore channels would increase the sterichindrance effects and disturb the continuous growth of the MOFs along [001] direction (Figure 2g, Figure S13). Therefore, the BTB molecules selectively ‘‘passivate’’ the crystal plane (001) of Ln-BTC MOFs crystal, while the π-π stacking between phenyl groups of BTC ligands along [100] and [010] directions will contribute to lateral growth of the Ln-BTC microstructures (Figure 2h). The introduction of the BTB molecules with one phenyl group into the channels could enhance the π-π interactions between BTC precursors and the trapped benzene ring from BTB. Therefore, the [010] and [100] directions are the two prominent crystal growth directions, further increasing the widths of Ln-BTC MOFs with increasing the concentration of the BTB modulator and eventually leading to the formation of 2D microplates.
## Assembly of Tunable Lateral Heterostructures
Below depicts that tunable Ln-MOFs microstructures presented above can serve as templates to construct epitaxial heterostructures with domain-controlled emission colors from different Ln ions. In a typical procedure, Tb-MOFs with different aspect ratios were initially synthesized, which are used as seeds for the growth of MOFs incorporated with a second Ln ion. Then the precursor solution of $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ ions was subsequently added into the suspension of the as-prepared Tb-MOFs microcrystals (Figure 3a). The isostructural Ln-MOFs with high crystallinity is beneficial for the epitaxial growth of the Eu-MOFs along the TbMOF skeleton (Figure S14).[7] After aging for 2 hours, the hybrid Ln-MOFs microstructures were finally obtained. Depending on the morphology of the Ln-MOFs seeds, MOFs heterostructures can be obtained with different aspect ratios (Figure 3b-3e). These lateral heterostructures exhibit two regions with obvious optical contrast and distinct photoluminescence properties corresponding to the the $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ ions, respectively (Figure 3f-h, Figure S15). The SEM image indicates that the Ln-MOFs heterostructures exhibit smooth surface (Figure S16), confirming the strong coordination interaction at interfaces without lattice mismatch.
According to the step-by-step assembly process, the emissive color of each segment strongly depends on its composition. Therefore, each sector can be chemically and spatially addressed independently and orthogonally by adjusting the emissive centers in each segment.[21] For instance, the green@red concentric lateral heterostructures can be further acquired by changing the addition sequence of the Ln ions precursors into the reaction (Figure 3i). In addition, PL properties of the Ln-MOFs with co-doped $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ can be effectively modulated through controlling the relative ratio of $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ in the host frameworks (Figure S17-18), which allows for generation of abundant color combinations for constructing multicolor heterojunctions.[22] Here, Ln-MOFs showing highly efficient orange luminescence were fabricated by co-doping $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ ions into the Tb-BTC host microplates (Figure S19). Tb/EuMOFs compounds can be epitaxially grown to encapsulate EuMOFs seeds, resulting in the formation of the orange@red concentric heterostructures (Figure 3j-k).
Benefiting from isostructural properties between various LnMOFs, further epitaxial growth of Ln-MOFs upon two-segment concentric heterostructures is feasible to create multi-segment concentric heterostructures (Figure 4a). For instance, threesegment concentric heterostructures can be achieved by stepwise feeding Tb-MOFs seeding crystals with $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{T b}^{3+}$ precursors subsequently, giving green/red/green emissions (Figure 4b). Likewise, various three-segment concentric heterostructures with different emission colors could be acquired by adjusting the emissive centers in each segment (Figure 4c-d). Such delicate engineering of individual emissive domains in MOFs heterostructures serves as the solid base for advanced applications described below.
## Anti-counterfeiting Application of Lateral Ln-MOFs Heterostructures
It is known that Ln-MOFs heterostructures serve as promising photonic barcoding due to spatially resolved narrowlined spectral features arising from Ln ions. Multi-segment LnMOFs microplate heterostructures are natually thought to outperform 1D microrords for photonic barcoding applications since they offer one additional dimension for barcoding thus improve the security level. A representative three-segment concentric Ln-MOFs heterostructures with three distinct reading lanes along the x and y axis is depicted in Figure 4e. These individual lanes in the microplates correspond to 1D blocks extracted from the planar structure of the microplates. By scanning and reading the PL spectra and the structure information in each lane, we can identify and authenticate planar 2D codes through an all-photonic approach (Figure S20).
Such unique segmented Ln-MOFs microstructure is beneficial to integrate the spectrum-based spectroscopic encoding with structure-based graphical encoding (Figure S21- 23).[6c, 23] The barcodes in an individual 1D block are defined as follows: each solid bar is located in the wavelength position, and the bar width is defined by the relative PL intensity of the same wavelength. Therefore, the PL spectrum from each segment can be expediently converted into a specific barcode. Then the subbarcode in each segment is compressed with the corresponding block length ratio to constitute multicolor built-up barcodes. Therefore, various 1D barcodes (Barcode 1-3) are effectively integrated into the 2D microplates heterojunction where multiple encoding elements could be utilized to enable the digital identification. Considering the diversity and flexibility of the multisegmented heterostructures, these tiny barcodes are expected to be expediently identifiable and difficult to replicate, which are superior to conventional techniques due to its digital identification nature and microscopic feature, showing great potential to function as the “security tag” toward improved anticounterfeiting applications.
The potential application of the developed lateral Ln-MOFs microplate heterostructures as security labels is proposed in Figure 4f-g. As a proof-of-concept illustration, a three-segment concentric Ln-MOF heterostructure was embedded into a bank card as the “security tag” by the manufacturer (Figure 4f). The corresponding PL spectrum and length information in different reading lanes were recorded from the planar Ln-MOFs heterostructure. According to the coding rule, the distinct barcodes (Barcode 1-3) can be acquired and were further input into a cloud for future reference (Figure 4g), and then the bank card, together with the tag, went into circulation (green arrow). The Barcode 1’-3’ can be obtained by means of encoder from the heterostructure and input into the cloud for an online inquiry. Only when Barcode 1-3 and Barcode 1’-3’ are satisfied simultaneously, the matching result is TRUE. Otherwise, the card is considered counterfeit.
In summary, we have reported a strategy to construct laterally tunable Ln-MOFs heterostructures through spatial semiconfinement induced steric-hindrance effect. Ln-MOFs microplates with different aspect ratios have been successfully generated through rebalancing the in-plane and out-of-plane growth rates of the Ln-MOFs seeds. Furthermore, various LnMOFs heterostructures were acquired via a stepwise epitaxial growth procedure, which exhibit domain-controlled emissive colors and identifiable dimensional tunable lengths, thus allowing for creating spatially resolved planar 2D photonic barcodes. The present work establishes new design and engineering principles for assembling multifunctional lateral LnMOFs heterostructures that exhibit compositional, spatial, and functional control at the micro/nanoscale.
